{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: forecasts are systematically below realized prices, especially from ~day 10 onward. Apply an upward level shift (≈ +0.5 to +1.5 €/t early horizon; rising to ≈ +2 to +5 €/t by days 20–30).",
    "Underreaction to uptrends/rallies: when the last 5–10 days show a rebound (e.g., from low 60s to mid/high 60s), the forecast path remains too flat/low. Add a trend-following adjustment proportional to recent slope (e.g., add 30–50% of the last-7-day linear trend to the next 2–3 weeks).",
    "Horizon-dependent drift error: forecast error increases with horizon (small at days 1–5, larger at days 15–30). Use a linearly increasing correction with lead time rather than a constant offset.",
    "Missed late-horizon spikes: the model fails to capture sharp upside moves near the end of the window (days ~23–30). Add a volatility/risk-premium uplift when recent realized volatility is elevated (e.g., if 10-day stdev rises, increase days 20–30 by an additional +1 to +3 €/t).",
    "Mean-reversion too strong after dips: following drawdowns in history, the model reverts to low/mid 60s while outcomes rebound to mid/high 60s. Reduce mean-reversion strength when the most recent price is near the 30-day low (i.e., allow higher rebound amplitude)."
  ]
}
